It gets dark very soon. In a few hours, you can see only the nearest trees, lit by the fire, and all the rest behind them is hidden in pitch darkness.
You are all tired and scared, but it's difficult to fall asleep. The jungle outside is full of weird and dreadful sounds. In the thickets, unknown animals howl and scream in fear, and you can often hear strange rustles. Some apelike creatures come down from the trees and look at your fire with curiosity. You look at them.
Suddenly, these creatures begin to scream loudly, frightened. One by one, they rush back up into the trees with lightning speed. For a moment, silence falls upon the jungle. Then, branches on the trees are pushed apart, and a large, hairy, coal-black beast steps out onto the glade.
Its long, messy hair hangs down, with its saber-like fangs gleaming in the light of the fire. The beast stands still, looking at your fire. The creature's nostrils move; it obviously senses you.
The predator comes closer, then stops, hesitating, as if some unseen barrier restrained it. A few tedious minutes pass, which seem like an eternity. Finally, the monster roars with disappointment, turns around, and goes back into the thicket.
Your heart is thumping madly. Then tiredness takes over, and you start to fall asleep. Your last thought is that you ought to sleep in turns to keep the fire.